                        "form": "primordia"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "30423e69-d71d-3acc-a55d-086f39ea5067",
                        "entry_id": "6712e790-d01c-33e4-8ed5-dff10da3f0d4",
                        "glosses": [
                            "An aggregation of cells that is the first stage in the development of an organ."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "606c4fa4-61d5-4384-8e21-f008597464d9",
                                "sense_id": "30423e69-d71d-3acc-a55d-086f39ea5067",
                                "text": "In tomato, for example, the first sepal primordium is formed as an outgrowth at the periphery of the apex and the subsequent sepal primordia are initiated in a helical fashion at an angle of approximately 137° from the previous one."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a06486bf-765a-35b6-b6f1-4393feb2fe5e",
                        "entry_id": "6712e790-d01c-33e4-8ed5-dff10da3f0d4",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A primordial, original condition or event."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "947fedcb-b05f-42e8-8493-3ff381d4190d",
                                "sense_id": "a06486bf-765a-35b6-b6f1-4393feb2fe5e",
                                "text": "It is thus vain to hope that the history of the primordia can be reduced to the progress of archeological investigation."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e11dcb42-a22f-4ccc-b070-d3bc2636d117",
                                "sense_id": "a06486bf-765a-35b6-b6f1-4393feb2fe5e",
                                "text": "Attempts have been made by historians of religion to distinguish between mythic orientations in time concerned with the repetition of a primordium and those concerned with the anticipation of an eschaton."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ebe62b67-8f89-46c6-ac14-83f52ea0eb82",
                                "sense_id": "a06486bf-765a-35b6-b6f1-4393feb2fe5e",
                                "text": "[Joseph Smith] did not think of himself as going back to a primordium of true Christianity, as the Puritans did. In his view, there never was a golden age when religion flourished to perfection."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
